.TH FCNTL 2 "2026-10-09" "Substrate" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
fcntl \- manipulate file descriptor flags and status flags
.SH LIBRARY
Substrate libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <fcntl.h>

int fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fcntl ()
operates on an existing file descriptor.
Substrate currently implements the basic descriptor and status-flag commands:
.TP
.B F_DUPFD
Duplicate
.I fd
to the lowest-numbered free descriptor greater than or equal to the supplied third argument.
The duplicate shares the same underlying file object and file offset, but its descriptor flags start clear.
.TP
.B F_GETFD
Return the descriptor flags for
.IR fd .
Only
.B FD_CLOEXEC
is currently defined.
.TP
.B F_SETFD
Set the descriptor flags for
.IR fd .
Only
.B FD_CLOEXEC
is honored.
.TP
.B F_GETFL
Return the file status flags and access mode for
.IR fd .
Substrate reports
.BR O_RDONLY ,
.BR O_WRONLY ,
or
.BR O_RDWR ,
plus
.BR O_APPEND
and
.BR O_NONBLOCK
when set.
.TP
.B F_SETFL
Update the mutable file status flags for
.IR fd .
Only
.B O_APPEND
and
.B O_NONBLOCK
are changed; access-mode bits are ignored.
.P
Descriptors marked with
.B FD_CLOEXEC
are closed on successful
.BR execve (2)
for native, Linux, and ELKS execution paths.
.SS Record locks
.BR F_SETLK ,
.B F_SETLKW
and
.B F_GETLK
take a pointer to a
.BR "struct flock" ,
which names a range of bytes by
.IR l_whence ,
.I l_start
and
.I l_len
(0 for "to the end of the file, however it grows") and a kind of lock in
.IR l_type :
.BR F_RDLCK ,
which any number of processes may hold on a byte at once,
.BR F_WRLCK ,
which one may and only while none reads, or
.B F_UNLCK
to take a lock off.  The locks are advisory: they keep out other lockers,
not readers and writers.
.TP
.B F_SETLK
Set or take off the lock, failing with
.B EAGAIN
if another process's lock is in the way.
.TP
.B F_SETLKW
The same, but wait for the way to be clear.  A signal that is caught ends
the wait with
.BR EINTR .
.TP
.B F_GETLK
Say whether the lock described could be set.  If so
.I l_type
comes back
.BR F_UNLCK ;
if not, the structure comes back describing one lock that prevents it, with
its owner in
.IR l_pid .
.P
A lock is on the
.I file
and belongs to the
.IR process .
Processes contend for a file whichever way each opened it; a process's own
locks are never in its own way, and setting a lock over part of one it
holds replaces that part.  A child does not inherit its parent's locks.
All of a process's locks on a file are given up when it closes
.I any
descriptor for that file, and when it exits.
.P
A file is known by its filesystem and inode number.  A lock on something
with no such identity (a device) is seen only by the processes that share
the open file description it was set through.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR fcntl ()
returns a value specific to
.IR cmd .
For
.BR F_DUPFD ,
the new descriptor number is returned.
For the other currently implemented commands, 0 or the requested flag word is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I fd
does not name an open file descriptor.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I cmd
is unsupported, or the argument is invalid for that command.
.TP
.B EMFILE
No free descriptor was available for
.BR F_DUPFD .
.TP
.B EAGAIN
.BR F_SETLK :
another process holds a lock that conflicts.
.TP
.B EDEADLK
.BR F_SETLKW :
the process whose lock is in the way is itself waiting, directly or through
others, for a lock this process holds.
.TP
.B EINTR
.BR F_SETLKW :
a signal was caught during the wait.
.TP
.B EFAULT
The
.B struct flock
is not in the caller's address space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dup (2),
.BR dup2 (2),
.BR execve (2),
.BR open (2)
